@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-add-typescript-toolchain-and-shared-jsonvalue-type-for-json-pointer
+type: decision-log
+title: "Add TypeScript toolchain and shared JsonValue type for JSON Pointer"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The repository had a `tsconfig.json` (strict, NodeNext, declaration output) but no `typescript`
+devDependency and no way to actually run `tsc`. Node 22's native TypeScript execution let
+`node --test` run `src/*.test.ts` directly, but that only strips types — it never type-checks.
+The code-quality directive requires public interfaces to carry explicit types and that static checks
+report no errors, which isn't meaningfully satisfiable without a working `tsc`. Separately,
+`resolvePointer` needed a type for "any value that can appear in a parsed JSON document," and the
+README says this is the first of several pointer-based features that build on each other.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Added `typescript` and `@types/node` as devDependencies, plus `typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`) and
+  `build` (`tsc`) npm scripts.
+- Set `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions: true` and `rootDir: "src"` in `tsconfig.json` so relative
+  imports can keep the `.ts` extension Node's native execution needs, while `tsc`'s build output still
+  gets `.js` extensions; added `types: ["node"]` so `node:test`/`node:assert` resolve.
+- Defined and exported `JsonValue` (recursive `string | number | boolean | null | JsonValue[] |
+  { [key: string]: JsonValue }`) from `src/index.ts` instead of using `unknown`/`any`, so
+  `resolvePointer` and the features planned next share one JSON value type.
+- `resolvePointer` throws plain `Error`s with descriptive messages rather than a custom error
+  subclass; nothing in this feature yet needs to distinguish "invalid pointer syntax" from
+  "pointer does not resolve" programmatically.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `npm run typecheck` and `npm run build` now work and pass cleanly; `npm test` continues to run the
+  `.ts` sources directly via Node's native execution, unaffected by the build output.
+- Later features that need to tell a syntax error apart from a resolution failure will have to
+  introduce distinct error types then; today's change does not preclude it.
+- `JsonValue` is a load-bearing export now: changing its shape later is a breaking change for whatever
+  consumes it.
